# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# vercel
.vercel

# local-only source assets (large / unreviewed originals, never deployed)
/_source-assets

# Source decks dropped at the repo root for extraction. "For website.pptx" is
# 445MB and carries patient identifiers burned into the images on several
# slides — a Heidelberg report with DOB, sex and patient ID, and an Optos frame
# with DOB and the treating site. Committing it would put PHI in git history,
# where removing it means rewriting history.
/*.pptx
/*.ppt

# Unfiltered deck extractions staged for review. Files here are raw clinical
# exports: many carry burned-in patient identifiers (name, DOB, MRN) and are
# captures from third-party devices. Anything in /public is served publicly, so
# this folder stays ignored until images have been de-identified, rights-cleared
# and moved to a reviewed location. See MANIFEST.csv (overlay_text column).
/public/case-library

# Full-resolution capture originals (13MB of 4073px JPEGs). The web-optimised
# WebP frames generated from these live in /public/fundus/spectral and are the
# only versions the site serves.
/public/fundus/OD/aligned

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Working artifacts from review sessions, not project source. output/pdf holds a
# 30MB team-review PDF and tmp/pdfs its page captures and build script; both are
# regenerable and neither is deployed. The files stay on disk, just out of git.
/output
/tmp

# Claude Code session state (lock files, scheduled tasks) — machine-local.
/.claude

# (The Vercel CLI appends `.vercel` and `.env*` here on `vercel link` / `env
# pull`. Both are already covered above, so this block is left as a marker
# rather than a second set of rules.)
